.TH SUM 1
.SH NAME
sum \- sum and count blocks in a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sum
[ file ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sum
calculates and prints a 16-bit checksum for the named files,
or for the standard input if no name appears,
and also prints the number of blocks in each.
It is typically used to look for bad spots, or
to validate a file communicated over
some transmission line.
.PP
.B Note:
the checksum is
.B still 16 bits
and is bit for bit the one a PDP\-11 computed for the same file, although a word on this
machine is 48.
The algorithm rotates a sixteen-bit accumulator right, adds the byte and masks back to
sixteen bits, and because the mask is applied on every byte rather than left to the
register's width, a wider machine cannot diverge from a narrower one.
.PP
.B Note:
the file name is printed only when
.B more than one
file was named.
A single named file gets a checksum and a block count and nothing else, which is v7's test \-
.B "argc > 2,"
not
.B "argc > 1."
.SH "BLOCKS ARE 1024 BYTES"
.B "Note:"
the block
.I sum
counts in is
.B 1024
bytes.
It is not this machine's filesystem block, which is
.B 3072
bytes \- 512 words of 48 bits \- and is three of them.
.PP
So every number
.I sum
prints is a
.B multiple of three,
the smallest thing that can be allocated being one 3072-byte block: a one-byte file reads as
three.
And the numbers are
.B twice
a PDP\-11's, v7 having counted in 512-byte blocks.
.PP
.IR Du (1),
.IR df (1M),
.IR quot (1M)
and
.IR ls (1)
report in the same block.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
`Read error'
is indistinguishable from end of file on
most devices; check the block count.
.PP
A file that cannot be opened adds 10 to the exit status and a read error adds 1, so a run
that failed to open one file exits 10.
That is v7's arithmetic, unchanged.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
wc(1), cmp(1), du(1)
